<p>Simmons was a projected top-15 pick out of Mississippi State, but he never tested at the combine. He wasn't invited, and in February 2019 he tore the ACL in his left knee during position drills while training for the draft. The numbers on record come from his pro day: <strong>6-foot-3¾, 305 pounds, 34½-inch arms, a 10¼-inch hand and a wingspan just under 6-foot-11</strong>, plus 28 reps of 225 pounds on the bench.</p>
<p>Tennessee drafted him 19th overall anyway, knowing he would miss a large chunk of his rookie year. He made his NFL debut on October 20, 2019, eight months after the injury, and has since become one of the best interior defenders in football: four Pro Bowls, second-team All-Pro in 2021 and 2022, and first-team All-Pro in 2025.</p>
<h3>Why arm length matters on the interior</h3>
<p>For a defensive tackle, the first contact decides the rep. The player who lands his hands first gets to control the other man's chest, and arm length is a direct head start. A 34½-inch arm is long for a 305-pound tackle. It lets Simmons strike an offensive guard and lock him out before the guard can grab him, then shed when the ball carrier or quarterback commits.</p>
