<p>The double-team numbers are the ones that stand out. Offenses routinely slide two blockers onto an elite interior rusher, which usually shuts his production down and frees up someone else. Simmons is still producing pressure and sacks through that extra attention, which means he makes the rest of a defensive line better just by lining up.</p>
<h2>What Strength Looks Like Without a 40 Time</h2>
<p>It's tempting to see a missing combine as a blank profile. For an interior lineman, it isn't. A defensive tackle almost never runs 40 yards in a straight line; his work happens in the first two or three steps after the snap. The traits that matter most there are length, hand strength, lower-body power and leverage, and Simmons' pro day numbers and seven seasons of tape both point the same way.</p>
<p>The ACL recovery is part of the story too. Coming back from a February knee reconstruction to play by October of his rookie year, and then building a first-team All-Pro résumé on that knee, says a lot about the strength base underneath the measurements.</p>
<div class="callout">
<div class="callout-icon">🏋️</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Training takeaway:</strong> For linemen and other power athletes, the first three steps matter more than the full 40. Hip and leg strength drive that initial burst, which is why squat strength and broad jump distance track so closely with short-area explosiveness. Our <a href="/squat-strength-sprint-speed">squat strength and sprint speed guide</a> explains the connection.</div>
</div>
<h2>First Up: Monday Night in Seattle</h2>
<p>Simmons joins a 5-2 Bears team chasing a second straight NFC North title. His first two games in Chicago are in prime time: Monday night at Seattle, then Sunday night against Tampa Bay. At his introductory press conference, Simmons said he was still learning the playbook and wasn't sure how much he'd play Monday. He also said the trade "told me everything I needed to know, especially the value from giving away two first-round picks."</p>
